  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Aureoboletus innixus, commonly known as the clustered brown bolete, is a fungal species in the boletes family characterized by its brown coloring and tendency to fruit in clusters or groups. This mushroom is found in New England and other parts of eastern North America, where it plays an ecological role in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Clustered Growth",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies typically grow in tight clusters or groups, sometimes with caps touching or partially fused together, which is highly distinctive for this species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown Cap and Stem",
      "description": "The mushroom features a brown to tan cap and a brown, often reticulated (netted) stem that may have yellowish tones at the base.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow Pores",
      "description": "The pores underneath the cap are yellow when young, becoming greenish-yellow with age, and bruise blue when handled or damaged.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Aureoboletus innixus grows on the forest floor in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests of New England, often appearing near rotting wood or in association with trees like oaks and conifers.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The clustered growth habit of this bolete is unusual among boletes, making it instantly recognizable to experienced foragers and mycologists, though the species remains relatively understudied compared to other boletes.",
